@title
Labeled Tuple Projection

@flags
show_report version=1 id=a89174ca-034d-43d7-b478-330388a2d712

@prompt
Hazel's labeled tuples support record-style projection:

```hazel
let pet = (name="Fido", age=4, species="dog") in
pet.name
```

# Task

Implement a function:
```hazelnostatics
greeting : Person -> String
```

The greeting should be of the form `Hello {first_name} {last_name}!`. Use `++` to concatenate strings.

**Example:**
```hazelnostatics
greeting((first_name="Jane", last_name="Smith", age=20))
== "Hello Jane Smith!"
```

@hint
Define a function and use projection with string concatenation to build the string

@reference
### Function Definition
```hazel
fun n -> n + 1 # Increments n by 1 #
```

### Binding and Calling a Function
```hazel
let inc : Int -> Int =
fun n -> n + 1
in
inc(1)
```

### Labeled Tuple Projection
Access a field of a labeled tuple with `.`:
```hazel
let pet =
(name="Fido", 
age=4, 
species="dog") in
pet.name
```


### String Concatenation
```hazel
"hello" ++ " world"
```

@hints
Don't forget spaces
Don't forget the exclamation point!

@code
type Person = (first_name=String, last_name=String, age=Int) in
let greeting : (Person -> String) = ¿ in
greeting((first_name="George", last_name="Garcia", age=12))

@test
test greeting((first_name="Jane", last_name="Smith",age=20)) == "Hello Jane Smith!" end;
test greeting((first_name="John", last_name="Baker", age=25)) == "Hello John Baker!" end
